If a patient has an opioid-use-disorder then an opioid-use-disorder-counseling can possibly benefit the patient.
If a clinician offers an opioid-use-disorder-counseling to a patient then the clinician should not replace an evidence-based-medication-treatment for the patient with an opioid-use-disorder-counseling.
If a patient has an opioid-use-disorder then a mutual-help-group-referral can possibly benefit the patient.
If a clinician offers a mutual-help-group-referral to a patient then the clinician should not replace an evidence-based-medication-treatment for the patient with a mutual-help-group-referral.
